course: dictionary
lesson: 13454
title: Huddled: close together
level: primary-4
stars: 0
explain: Huddled means pressed close together in a group. Look at the clues around the word: the rest of the sentence tells you why, such as cold, fear or a secret.
explain: «The children huddled under a blanket during the storm.» The blanket and the storm are the clues. The children were close together, probably to keep warm and feel safe.
explain: «The emperor penguins huddled together against the icy wind.» The icy wind is the clue. Try your meaning in the sentence: "The penguins crowded close together against the icy wind" still makes sense, so you have it.
explain: Huddled can also mean curled up with arms and legs pulled in. «Sam huddled in the corner, afraid of the dark.» The corner and afraid are the clues that Sam was curled up and scared.
fact: Huddled means crowded closely together in a group, often to keep warm, feel safe or talk secretly.
fact: Huddled can also mean curled up or hunched, with arms and legs pulled close to the body.
fact: As an adjective, huddled describes things pressed close together, like «huddled houses» or «huddled sheep».
fact: Animals huddle to save body heat; emperor penguins are a famous example.
fact: Useful phrases: huddled together, huddled around a fire, huddled up, huddled in a corner.
exercise: «The children huddled under a blanket during the storm.» What does huddled mean here? | Shouting loudly at the rain | Sleeping alone in a big bed | Running fast across a field | *Crowded close together to keep warm and safe
exercise: «The team huddled to plan their next play.» What is the team doing when it huddles? | Dancing on the pitch | *Planning a play together | Eating lunch | Leaving the game early
exercise: «Mia huddled in the corner, shivering with fear.» What does huddled suggest about Mia? | *Curled up and frightened | Happy and playing a game | Running to the shop | Lost on her way home
test: «Huddled houses» means houses that are | Made of stone | Tall and far apart | *Pressed close together | Painted in bright colours
test: Why do emperor penguins huddle? | *To save body heat in the icy cold | To hunt for fish in the sea | To dance for the crowd | To hide from the sun
exam: Which sentence uses huddled to mean pressed close together? | The cake was left on the table all day | *The families huddled around the campfire to stay warm | The boat sailed fast across the bay | The shop was closed on Sunday
